  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will impact at least Downeast Maine\nthis weekend into Monday and possibly Tuesday, bringing gusty \nwinds, high surf, and periods of rain.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWe are still expecting a strong Noreaster to impact our area\nfrom through the weekend and into Monday. However, we \nwill be on the far northern fringe of the Noreaster with the \ncenter staying south of Massachusetts through Sunday, and \nimpacts in Northern regions through Sunday will be much less than \nareas further south. Here is what to expect for the various \ncomponents of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Rain chances will be far greater Downeast than\nnorthern areas for the storm, with rain possibly developing late\nSaturday and Saturday night, and persisting off and on through \nMonday and possibly even Tuesday. Short range model guidance \nhas adjusted the track of the system to remain much further \nsouth than previous thought. As a result, periods of rain should\nreach the Downeast coast by late Saturday afternoon, extending \nacross Bangor and the interior Downeast by Saturday night. Rain \nfall totals have coalesced somewhat in with the updated track. \nPrecipitation amounts between 0.25 inches and 1 inch are \nexpected across the Bangor region with localized totals up to \n1.5 inches along the coast. Much of this will wait until Sunday\nnight and Monday to occur. Even with these totals, we would not\nexpect flooding. Sunday night and Monday, a much weaker version\nof the Noreaster finally lifts north and tracks into the Gulf \nof Maine, at which point rain chances and totals finally \nincrease over the whole area. Northern Maine could finally see \nsome rain late Monday into Tuesday, but it looks like it will be\nfairly light with maybe around one quarter inch.\n\nGusty Winds...Winds from the northeast will increase Friday\nnight into Saturday, before becoming gusty by Saturday and \nthrough Sunday, then decrease a bit into Monday. Winds will be \nstronger the further south you go but will top out around 25 mph\nacross Bangor and the Downeast with the aforementioned \nsoutherly track of the system, with isolated higher amounts \nalong the coast. We don't anticipate impacts from the wind other\nthan maybe a very isolated outage Downeast. \n\nHigh Surf...Seas near the shore will be fairly rough with 4-8 \nft seas, much of it as a longer period south/southeast swell. We\nare not expecting impactful coastal flooding, though waves \nbreaking on the rocks will be hazardous for any onlookers. \nConditions should be a bit more hazardous for coastal Hancock \nthan coastal Washington. Conditions slowly ease Sunday night \ninto Monday.",
